    apply: "Identify one area where you hear the question 'Did God actually say?' this week, such as honesty, purity or generosity. Find the verse that answers it, write it on a card and memorise it. When you are tempted, say it aloud and pray.",
    activity: "Divide into three groups, each taking one of the serpent's three steps. Each group lists modern examples of that step, such as advertisements, peer pressure and social media. Report back and read Matthew 4:1-11 to see how Jesus answered. Close with prayer for strength.",
    takeHome: "Read Genesis 3:1-5 and Matthew 4:1-11. Make a table comparing how the serpent tempted Eve and how he tempted Jesus, and how each responded. Memorise 1 Peter 5:8-9.",
    prayer: "Ask God to help us know his word and to recognise deception. Pray for protection from the evil one. Thank Jesus who defeated temptation for us.",
    teacher: "Avoid unhealthy fascination with the devil. Keep the focus on God's word and on how deception works. Learners may have experienced fear linked to spiritual attack, so assure them that Christ is stronger and that the Spirit lives in believers.",
    assess: "Weekly check: ask learners to name the three methods used by the serpent and to give one way to resist.",
    mcq1: ["What was the serpent's first question in Genesis 3:1?", ["Did God actually say?", "Who are you?", "Where is Adam?", "Are you hungry?"], 0, "He began by casting doubt on God's word."],
    mcq2: ["What did the serpent say would happen if they ate?", ["You will not surely die", "You will become wealthy", "You will fall asleep", "You will leave the garden"], 0, "He directly contradicted God's warning."],
    tf: ["Satan's temptations often appear as openly evil invitations.", false, "They usually appear as questions and attractive promises."],
    sa: ["Describe two methods the serpent used to tempt Eve.", ["doubt", "deny", "promise", "word", "god", "lie"], "A good answer names casting doubt on God's word, denying the consequences, slandering God's motives or promising what only God can give."],
