    summary: "This session reviews lessons 1 to 12: the definition of sin, the good beginning, the temptation and the fall, shame and blame, God's seeking, the consequences, the promise, exile, Cain and Abel, the spread of sin and original sin. It prepares learners for the mid-session test.",
    scripture: ["Romans 3:23", "Genesis 3:15"], more: ["Genesis 3:1-6", "Romans 5:12", "1 John 3:8"], memory: "Romans 5:19",
    minutes: 60,
    objectives: ["Recall the main truths about the origin and nature of sin from weeks 1 to 12", "Link each truth to a passage of Scripture", "Prepare for the mid-session test with confidence and hope"],
    terms: [["Fall", "The first disobedience that brought sin and death into the world."], ["Original sin", "The sinful condition inherited from Adam."], ["Promise of the Deliverer", "God's pledge in Genesis 3:15 of a descendant who would defeat the serpent."]],
    intro: "We have spent twelve weeks studying one of the hardest subjects in the Bible. We have seen what sin is, how it entered the world, how it spread and what it did. We have also seen that at every step God sought, warned, promised and provided. Today we gather those threads, review them and prepare for the test, remembering that the point of understanding sin is to appreciate grace.",
    context: "This is the midpoint of the programme and the default week of the mid-session test, which covers lessons 1 to 12. Churches may hold the test during class time or on another day; where it is on another day, this session serves as a guided review. Romans 3:23 and Genesis 3:15 together sum up the first half: all have sinned, and God has promised a Saviour.",
    observe: "Paul's summary in Romans 3:23 states the universal reach of sin, and verse 24 immediately adds that we are justified by his grace as a gift, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. The first half of this course has focused on the problem. Genesis 3:15 reminds us that the solution was announced at the very beginning.",
    points: [
      ["Sin is rebellion against God that entered through Adam and Eve", "The serpent questioned God's word, the couple desired and ate, and sin brought shame, hiding, blame, exile and death. We reviewed 1 John 3:4, Romans 3:23, Genesis 3 and Romans 5:12. Sin is first an offence against God, and every person is affected by it."],
      ["Sin spreads and corrupts every part of life", "From Cain's murder to Lamech's boast to the corruption before the flood, sin multiplied. We looked at Genesis 4 and 6, and at original sin in Romans 5 and Psalm 51. All people are born with a sinful condition, and we cannot save ourselves without a Saviour."],
      ["God seeks, warns, judges and promises", "God asked 'Where are you?', clothed the couple, warned Cain and showed favour to Noah. The promise of Genesis 3:15 points to Christ who crushes the serpent. The second half of the course will look at the many forms of sin and how the gospel answers them."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Which lesson most changed how you think about sin?", "Let learners share. Use answers to review. Ask them to support answers from the passage."],
      ["How would you explain to a friend what happened in the garden and why it matters?", "Disobedience, loss, consequences and the promise. Let learners give clear, simple explanations."],
      ["Which sign of God's mercy in these twelve lessons encourages you most?", "Seeking, clothing, warning Cain, favour to Noah, the promise. Encourage specific, personal answers."],
      ["What questions do you still have?", "Record them for later sessions. Encourage practical questions from learners' own contexts."],
    ],
    apply: "Write a one-page summary of the first twelve lessons with a Scripture reference for each key idea. Review it twice before the test and teach one idea to someone at home. Thank God for his mercy in each lesson.",
    activity: "Play a team review game: the teacher reads a clue (for example 'crouching at the door') and teams call out the topic and a passage. Give extra points for application. End by reading Romans 3:23-24 together.",
    takeHome: "Prepare for the test by reviewing the revision map and memory verses. Write five practice questions of your own, with answers, and test someone.",
    prayer: "Thank God for his word and his mercy. Ask for a calm mind for the test. Pray that the truths we have learned will lead us to a deeper trust in Christ.",
    teacher: "Explain how the mid-session test works: time, number of questions, and when results are released. Reassure anxious learners. If the test is held on another date, use this session as a review. Close with prayer and encouragement.",
    assess: "This unit is the mid-session test week. The mid-session test covers Lessons 1 to 12 and is built from the approved question bank following the curriculum blueprint.",
    mcq1: ["Which verse says all have sinned and fall short of God's glory?", ["Romans 3:23", "John 3:16", "Psalm 23:1", "Genesis 1:1"], 0, "Romans 3:23 states it."],
